# The product pass

Loom's pipeline is a chain of small passes between explicit models. This module is one of them, and it is the one that composes: it folds a parsed `.loom` document — a `LoomDocument` of sections — straight into its `Product`, the author's own code with each `::[…]` anchor turned into an edge. A section's name anchors already form the document's composition graph, so the pass reads that graph off the parse and builds the product from it, rather than generating a program to compute the same result.

The pass is a build, the same shape as the parser before it. The parser folds a stream of wefts into the Loom AST; this folds that AST's sections into the product. It is total by construction. Every section becomes one `Code`, and a section that names a file to write — with a `[file]` sink, or a `{Tangle}` specifier — becomes a `File` as well. An anchor the pass cannot compose becomes an empty `Fragment` carrying error health on its own node: a fault the editor reports, never a thrown exception. Grammatical health, such as a malformed anchor, is already on the parse. Semantic health, such as an anchor that names two sections or crosses a language, is attached here, where the pass has the names to judge it.

What an anchor edge carries is an identity, not a copy. A `NameRef` records the `SectionId` its anchor resolves to and nothing more. `fromProduct` follows that edge across the corpus and inlines the target later, at projection.

So this pass stays within one document. A bare anchor whose name the document holds resolves against the document's own sections, and the pass judges it in full. Any other anchor reaches beyond the document, which this pass never sees. A name the document lacks may name a chapter elsewhere in the book, or a section through a path of names, so its edge records the name and leaves the target open. `crossFileFaults` judges such an edge once the compiler holds the whole corpus.

## What this module draws on

The pass reads the Loom AST and writes the product AST, so it draws from both. From `effect` come the array, option, and record combinators, `Match` for dispatching on a weft, `Order` for sorting a run's anchors by position, `Data` for the tagged answers a reference gets, `Result` for a step that may stop with one of them, and `pipe` for the spine.

From the Loom side come the document and section types, the warp and anchor tokens a section carries, the `ParametersToken` an anchor's parameters arrive in, each a `ParameterToken`, the `SpecifierToken` and `SinkToken` a heading marks its file with, `settleKind`, the kind that marks a section to be settled, `referenceKind`, the kind that marks a reference, and the section-body weft a code run is made of. The tokeniser's `normaliseTitle` folds a heading into the name that identifies its section.

The fault catalog gives `faulty` and the faults this pass raises. An anchor raises `UnresolvedAnchor`, with the `Declaration` it offers, `AmbiguousAnchor`, `ChapterWithoutCode`, `CrossLanguageAnchor`, `StrayTarget` on the `WarpAnchorTargetToken` it writes, and the `UnsettledSection` and `SettledLabel` notes. A reference raises `NoSuchFile`, `SeveralFiles`, `SectionWithoutFile`, and the `UnsettledModule` note. A section's name raises `DuplicateName`, a fence's label raises `FenceLabelAgainstLanguage` on the `FenceLabelToken`, a heading's marks raise `TangleBesideSink` and `TangleWithoutFile`, and a warp raises `UnknownVariable`.

From the product side come the schema constructors for every node the pass builds: the `Code` of a section, its `Fragment`, `NameRef`, and `ModuleRef` leaves, and the `File` a section emits. With them come the `SectionId` that stamps each node and `keyOf`, which names it, the `Provided` names an edge records, and `isMeant`, which asks whether an edge promises its section. From the corpus model come the `LoomModule` the judgement reads, the `Book` it resolves names in through `trailsOf`, `sectionOf`, and `resolveName`, the `Place` a trail ends at, and `pathsTo`, which names each candidate of an ambiguous name, with the `Diagnostic` the judgement returns.

=>

import { Array, Context, Data, Effect, Layer, Match, Option, Order, pipe, Record, Result } from 'effect'
import type {
  LoomDocument,
  LoomFrontmatter,
  LoomHeading,
  LoomSection,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import {
  okHealth,
  type Diagnostic,
  type Health,
  type Point,
  type Position,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import {
  type Book,
  type Location,
  type LoomModule,
  pathsTo,
  type Place,
  resolveName,
  sectionOf,
  type SectionRef,
  type Trail,
  trailsOf,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import {
  referenceKind,
  settleKind,
  type FenceLabelToken,
  type ParameterToken,
  type ParametersToken,
  type SinkToken,
  type SpecifierToken,
  type WarpAnchorTargetToken,
  type WarpAnchorToken,
  type WarpToken,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import type { SectionBodyWeft } from '@athrio/loom-ast/Weft'
import {
  type Code,
  CodeSchema,
  type File,
  FileSchema,
  type Fragment,
  FragmentSchema,
  isMeant,
  keyOf,
  type ModuleRef,
  ModuleRefSchema,
  type NameRef,
  NameRefSchema,
  type Part,
  type Product,
  ProductSchema,
  type Provided,
  type SectionId,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/ProductAst'
import {
  AmbiguousAnchor,
  ChapterWithoutCode,
  CrossLanguageAnchor,
  type Declaration,
  DuplicateName,
  faulty,
  FenceLabelAgainstLanguage,
  NoSuchFile,
  SectionWithoutFile,
  SettledLabel,
  SeveralFiles,
  StrayTarget,
  TangleBesideSink,
  TangleWithoutFile,
  UnsettledModule,
  UnsettledSection,
  UnknownVariable,
  UnresolvedAnchor,
} from '#ast/LoomFault'
import { normaliseTitle } from '#ast/WeftTokeniser'

## The builder and its spine

`ProductBuilder` is the service the rest of the pipeline calls. Its one method, `build`, wraps the pure `buildProduct` in an Effect so callers compose it like any other pass. It takes the document's file path alongside the document, because each section's identity is its path and name, and the path is known only to the caller. It also takes the package's primary language, the default a section inherits when the frontmatter declares no `Language`, which the caller resolves from the file's configuration.

`buildProduct` is the spine, and it runs from the document's head to its tail. It resolves the primary language and the output package from the frontmatter, indexes the sections by the name each anchor reaches them by, notes the first section that holds each name, then maps every section to a `Built` — its `Code`, and the `File` a `{Tangle}` section or a tangle sink adds — and folds those into the module's `Product`. `documentLanguage` and `documentPackage` read the primary language and the package from the frontmatter.

=>

export class ProductBuilder extends Context.Service<ProductBuilder>()('ProductBuilder', {
  make: Effect.succeed({
    build: (
      doc: LoomDocument,
      path: string,
      packageLanguage?: string,
      variables: Record<string, string> = {},
    ): Effect.Effect<Product> =>
      Effect.sync(() => buildProduct(doc, path, packageLanguage, variables)),
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make)
}

export const buildProduct = (
  doc: LoomDocument,
  modulePath: string,
  packageLanguage?: string,
  variables: Record<string, string> = {},
): Product => {
  const pkg = documentPackage(doc)
  const lang = documentLanguage(doc, packageLanguage)
  const index = nameIndex(doc.sections, lang, pkg)
  const first = firstHolders(doc.sections)
  return pipe(
    doc.sections,
    Array.map(buildSection(index, first, lang, modulePath, pkg, variables)),
    Array.reduce(emptyProduct, appendSection),
  )
}

const documentPackage = (doc: LoomDocument): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(doc.frontmatter),
    Option.flatMapNullishOr((f) => f.package),
    Option.map((p) => p.value),
  )

const documentLanguage = (doc: LoomDocument, packageLanguage?: string): string =>
  pipe(
    frontmatterLanguage(Option.fromNullishOr(doc.frontmatter)),
    Option.orElse(() => Option.fromNullishOr(packageLanguage)),
    Option.getOrElse(() => 'plaintext'),
  )

const emptyProduct: Product = ProductSchema.make({ code: [], files: [] })

const appendSection = (product: Product, built: Built): Product =>
  ProductSchema.make({
    code: [...product.code, built.code],
    files: [...product.files, ...Option.toArray(built.file)],
  })

## Resolving names

A name anchor reaches a section by its heading title — the one handle every section has. The index is keyed by that title, and each title maps to the section the title names: its title normalised to an identifier.

A title can repeat, and the index keeps every section that claims one rather than letting the last overwrite the rest. So a title maps to a *list* of sections, not a single one. A list of one is a clean resolution; a longer list is an ambiguity the anchor reports rather than guesses through.

The primary language is the one a specifier-less section inherits, resolved from three sources in order. The frontmatter's `Language` field sets it, lowercased to a language id. The field is optional: when the frontmatter omits it, the language falls back to the package's configured language, passed in as `packageLanguage`, and then to `plaintext` when the package configures none. `frontmatterLanguage` reads the field and yields nothing when it is absent, so the fallback chain walks the three sources in turn.

A tangle sink resolves its language a fourth way: it carries no specifier, only a path, so `extensionLanguage` reads the language off the file's name. It maps `.ts` and its siblings to `typescript`, `.tsx` to `tsx`, the JavaScript extensions to `javascript` and `jsx`, `.json` to `json`, `.md` to `markdown`, and `.yml` to `yaml`. Those last two are the short forms of names a frontmatter writes in full, so a Markdown chapter can anchor a section into its own `.md` sink. An unlisted extension falls back to itself, so a new language reads as its bare extension until a service claims it. A `.json` sink composes as JSON even in a TypeScript document.

A file with no extension takes its whole name as its language, read from the last path segment only. A `Dockerfile` composes `dockerfile`, a `LICENSE` composes `license`, and a `.gitignore`, whose one dot only marks it hidden, composes `gitignore`. A chapter that declares the same language can then anchor into it.

`sectionLanguage` resolves any section's language by these rules: its specifier, a sink's extension, or the document default. The weave labels each code block by the same rule, so a page names a section's language exactly as the tangler composes it. `nameIndex` records the language beside each title, so when an anchor binds, the pass compares the composing section's language to the section it reaches and marks a cross-language transclusion when they differ. It also records whether the section holds any code yet, so an anchor that reaches a section of prose alone is marked as composing a section to be settled.

=>

type NameEntry = {
  readonly name: string
  readonly pos: Position
  readonly language: string
  readonly written: boolean
}
type NameIndex = ReadonlyMap<string, ReadonlyArray<NameEntry>>

const specifierLanguage = (
  label: string,
  sink: Option.Option<SinkToken>,
  defaultLang: string,
  pkg: Option.Option<string>,
): string => {
  const id = label.toLowerCase()
  if (id === 'tangle')
    return Option.match(pkg, {
      onNone: () => defaultLang,
      onSome: (p) => extensionLanguage(tangleFilePath(p, sink)),
    })
  if (id === 'toc') return 'prose'
  return id
}

export const sectionLanguage = (
  section: LoomSection,
  defaultLang: string,
  pkg: Option.Option<string>,
): string => {
  const { specifier, sink } = section.heading
  if (specifier !== undefined)
    return specifierLanguage(specifier.label.value, Option.fromNullishOr(sink), defaultLang, pkg)
  if (sink === undefined) return defaultLang
  return extensionLanguage(sink.file.value)
}

const nameIndex = (
  sections: ReadonlyArray<LoomSection>,
  defaultLang: string,
  pkg: Option.Option<string>,
): NameIndex =>
  pipe(
    sections,
    Array.map((s) =>
      Option.map(
        Option.fromNullishOr(s.heading.title),
        (title) =>
          [
            title.source,
            {
              name: sectionNameOf(s),
              pos: title.position,
              language: sectionLanguage(s, defaultLang, pkg),
              written: Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(codeRuns(s.code)),
            },
          ] as const,
      ),
    ),
    Array.getSomes,
    Array.reduce(new Map<string, ReadonlyArray<NameEntry>>(), (index, [title, entry]) =>
      new Map(index).set(title, [...(index.get(title) ?? []), entry]),
    ),
  )

const frontmatterLanguage = (frontmatter: Option.Option<LoomFrontmatter>): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    frontmatter,
    Option.flatMapNullishOr((f) => f.language),
    Option.map((l) => l.value.trim().toLowerCase()),
  )

const languageByExtension: ReadonlyMap<string, string> = new Map([
  ['ts', 'typescript'],
  ['mts', 'typescript'],
  ['cts', 'typescript'],
  ['tsx', 'tsx'],
  ['js', 'javascript'],
  ['mjs', 'javascript'],
  ['cjs', 'javascript'],
  ['jsx', 'jsx'],
  ['json', 'json'],
  ['md', 'markdown'],
  ['yml', 'yaml'],
])

const extensionLanguage = (path: string): string => {
  const name = path.slice(path.lastIndexOf('/') + 1).toLowerCase()
  const dot = name.lastIndexOf('.')
  const ext = dot < 0 ? name : name.slice(dot + 1)
  return languageByExtension.get(ext) ?? ext
}

## Value warps

A warp binds a name its section's anchors compose. A value warp binds the name to a literal — a string, a number — and `::[n]` substitutes that value where the anchor stood. A value needs no edge and no section: the pass drops it in place. A quoted string yields its content — `{{kw = "const"}}` lands `const` — and the author writes any surrounding syntax, the quotes for a string slot, around the anchor itself.

A value may also draw on the workspace. Writing `${name}` in a value interpolates the workspace variable `name`, declared under `variables:` in the configuration, so a version or any value the whole corpus shares lives in one place. The reference resolves bare or inside a string, since the pass interpolates after it decodes the literal. A `${name}` that no variable declares stays in the text and raises an `UnknownVariable` on the warp, so a typo is loud rather than silent. Interpolation touches a warp's value alone, never a section's code, so a `${…}` a target language writes is left untouched.

`valueWarps` gathers them. It keeps each warp that carries a value, decodes and interpolates it, and maps the name to that text, its position, and its health. `decodeLiteral` unwraps a quoted string to its content and leaves a bare number as written, and `interpolate` then fills each `${name}` from the variables, so a warp resolves to a literal value, never an evaluated expression. A warp with no value binds nothing — a fault the tokeniser already raised. The document's `lang` warp carries an annotation rather than a value, so it never enters the map.

=>

interface ValueWarp {
  readonly text: string
  readonly pos: Position
  readonly health: Health
}

const decodeLiteral = (text: string): string => {
  const s = text.trim()
  const quoted = /^"(?:\\.|[^"\\])*"$/.test(s) || /^'(?:\\.|[^'\\])*'$/.test(s)
  return quoted ? s.slice(1, -1).replace(/\\(.)/g, (_, c) => c) : text
}

const variablePattern = /[$][{]([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)[}]/g

const interpolate = (
  text: string,
  variables: Record<string, string>,
): { readonly text: string; readonly missing: Option.Option<string> } => ({
  text: text.replace(variablePattern, (whole, name: string) =>
    name in variables ? variables[name]! : whole,
  ),
  missing: pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(text.matchAll(variablePattern)),
    Array.map((match) => match[1]!),
    Array.filter((name) => !(name in variables)),
    Array.head,
  ),
})

const valueWarps = (
  warps: ReadonlyArray<WarpToken>,
  variables: Record<string, string>,
): ReadonlyMap<string, ValueWarp> =>
  new Map(
    Array.getSomes(
      Array.map(warps, (w) =>
        Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(w.default), (def) => {
          const { text, missing } = interpolate(decodeLiteral(def.value), variables)
          return [
            w.name.value,
            {
              text,
              pos: def.position,
              health: Option.match(missing, {
                onNone: () => okHealth,
                onSome: (name) => faulty(UnknownVariable({ name }), def.position),
              }),
            },
          ] as const
        }),
      ),
    ),
  )

## Building one section

A section's heading decides what it contributes. Every section composes to one `Code`: its identity, its language, and its fragments in composition order. A section that names a file — through a `[file]` sink, or a `{Tangle}` specifier — contributes a `File` on top, the same `Code` paired with the path it tangles to.

A `Built` is the pair a section yields: its `Code`, which always lands in the product, and the `File` a `{Tangle}` section or a tangle sink adds, which an ordinary section leaves absent. `buildSection` gathers the section's value warps and interpolates each against the workspace variables, resolves its language and identity, and composes its code. The code carries a fault when an earlier section of the document already holds the section's name, when a fence's label names another language, or when the heading marks its file in a way the sink rule forbids.

=>

interface Built {
  readonly code: Code
  readonly file: Option.Option<File>
}

const buildSection =
  (
    index: NameIndex,
    first: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomSection>,
    defaultLang: string,
    modulePath: string,
    pkg: Option.Option<string>,
    variables: Record<string, string>,
  ) =>
  (section: LoomSection): Built => {
    const values = valueWarps(
      section.preamble.flatMap((w) => w.warps),
      variables,
    )
    const origin: SectionId = { path: modulePath, name: sectionNameOf(section) }
    const languageId = sectionLanguage(section, defaultLang, pkg)
    const health = Array.reduce(
      [
        duplicateHealth(first, section),
        labelHealth(section, languageId),
        tangleHealth(section, pkg),
      ],
      okHealth,
      withHealth,
    )
    const code = buildCode(section, values, index, origin, languageId, health)
    const file = tangleFile(section, pkg, code)
    return { code, file }
  }

~

`tangleFile` decides whether a section names a file, and `filePath` decides where. A heading names a file with one of two marks. A `[file]` sink names the file itself. A `{Tangle}` specifier names the chapter's one file, the file the frontmatter's `Package` names when it names a file rather than a folder, and `singleFile` reads that file. Any other specifier is a language label, so the section composes in place and writes nothing.

`filePath` resolves where a sink's file lands. Under a frontmatter `Package` the file lands there, where `tangleFilePath` appends the `[file]` tag to a directory package and passes a file package through unchanged. Under no package the sink names the file by itself, and the compiler resolves where it lands. So the smallest program that tangles is a heading with a sink, an arrow, and its code: `# Hi there! [mini.ts]` writes `mini.ts`, its language read from the extension.

=>

const tangleFilePath = (pkg: string, sink: Option.Option<SinkToken>): string =>
  pkg.endsWith('/')
    ? pkg +
      Option.getOrElse(
        Option.map(sink, (s) => s.file.value),
        () => '',
      )
    : pkg

const filePath = (
  pkg: Option.Option<string>,
  sink: Option.Option<SinkToken>,
): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.match(pkg, {
    onSome: (p) => Option.some(tangleFilePath(p, sink)),
    onNone: () => Option.map(sink, (s) => s.file.value),
  })

const singleFile = (pkg: Option.Option<string>): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.filter(pkg, (p) => !p.endsWith('/'))

const isTangle = (specifier: SpecifierToken): boolean =>
  specifier.label.value.toLowerCase() === 'tangle'

const tangleFile = (
  section: LoomSection,
  pkg: Option.Option<string>,
  code: Code,
): Option.Option<File> => {
  const sink = Option.fromNullishOr(section.heading.sink)
  const target = Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(section.heading.specifier), {
    onNone: () => Option.flatMap(sink, () => filePath(pkg, sink)),
    onSome: (spec) =>
      Match.value({ tangle: isTangle(spec), sunk: Option.isSome(sink) }).pipe(
        Match.when({ tangle: true, sunk: true }, () => filePath(pkg, sink)),
        Match.when({ tangle: true }, () => singleFile(pkg)),
        Match.orElse(() => Option.none<string>()),
      ),
  })
  return Option.map(target, (path) => FileSchema.make({ path, code }))
}

## One mark for one file

The sink and the `{Tangle}` specifier each say that a section writes a file, so a heading that carries both says it twice. `tangleHealth` holds a heading to one mark. A `{Tangle}` beside a sink raises the `TangleBesideSink` fault on the specifier, and the fault's fix removes the specifier, so `{Tangle}[room.ts]` becomes `[room.ts]`. Until the author takes the fix, the section writes the sink's file, so the editor goes on checking its code at the path it tangles to.

A `{Tangle}` with no sink means the chapter's one file. When the `Package` names a folder, or the chapter names no `Package` at all, the specifier has no file to mean. It raises the `TangleWithoutFile` fault, and the section writes nothing. The fault names the folder when there is one, so the author sees why the folder cannot stand for a file.

`removalOf` gives the span the fix removes: the specifier and the spaces that part it from the sink, so the heading keeps no doubled space.

=>

const tangleHealth = (section: LoomSection, pkg: Option.Option<string>): Health =>
  Option.match(Option.filter(Option.fromNullishOr(section.heading.specifier), isTangle), {
    onNone: () => okHealth,
    onSome: (specifier) =>
      Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(section.heading.sink), {
        onSome: (sink) =>
          faulty(
            TangleBesideSink({ sink: sink.source, specifier: removalOf(specifier, sink) }),
            specifier.position,
          ),
        onNone: () =>
          Option.match(singleFile(pkg), {
            onSome: () => okHealth,
            onNone: () =>
              faulty(
                TangleWithoutFile(
                  Option.match(pkg, { onNone: () => ({}), onSome: (folder) => ({ folder }) }),
                ),
                specifier.position,
              ),
          }),
      }),
  })

const removalOf = (specifier: SpecifierToken, sink: SinkToken): Position =>
  specifier.position.start.offset < sink.position.start.offset
    ? between(specifier.position.start, sink.position.start)
    : between(sink.position.end, specifier.position.end)

## Composing a section's code

`buildCode` turns a section's code into one `Code`. It walks each code run, binding each anchor against the value warps and the name index, and gathers the fragments and edges the runs yield. The `Code` carries the section's identity, its language, and the health its name earns; a section with no code composes an empty one.

A *code piece* is the unit of composition: one line from a code weft, or the inline code after an arrow, extended to the line's end so its trailing newline is kept. `pieceOf` lifts a weft to a piece, and a prose weft yields none.

A run sheds its blank lines at the edges but keeps them inside. The section's last run trims both edges, since it closes the section; every earlier run keeps a single trailing blank as its seam to the next code chunk. So a section that interleaves code and prose composes with one blank line at each seam, never a doubled gap. `codeRuns` splits the section into these runs, one per `=>` arrow, and discards the prose between them.

`walkRun` turns one run into its parts. The run's anchors, sorted by position, partition its text: each anchor contributes the fragment before it and then its edge, and a closing fragment runs to the end. Each fragment carries its `.loom` span as the origin it maps back to, and the text is the source slice itself — no escaping, since a fragment is data the projection reads, not a literal it must quote. Each boundary is computed from the anchor's index, so no cursor threads through the text.

=>

const buildCode = (
  section: LoomSection,
  values: ReadonlyMap<string, ValueWarp>,
  index: NameIndex,
  origin: SectionId,
  languageId: string,
  health: Health,
): Code => {
  const bind = bindAnchor(index, values, languageId, origin.path)
  const fragments = pipe(codeRuns(section.code), Array.flatMap(walkRun(bind)))
  return CodeSchema.make({ origin, languageId, fragments, health })
}

interface CodePiece {
  readonly source: string
  readonly position: Position
  readonly anchors: ReadonlyArray<WarpAnchorToken>
}

const pieceOf = (w: SectionBodyWeft): Option.Option<CodePiece> =>
  pipe(
    Match.value(w),
    Match.when({ type: 'CodeWeft' }, (c) =>
      Option.some({ source: c.source, position: c.position, anchors: c.anchors }),
    ),
    Match.when({ type: 'ArrowWeft' }, (a) =>
      Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(a.code), (code) => ({
        source: a.source.slice(code.position.start.offset - a.position.start.offset),
        position: between(code.position.start, a.position.end),
        anchors: a.anchors,
      })),
    ),
    Match.orElse(() => Option.none<CodePiece>()),
  )

const isBlank = (p: CodePiece): boolean => p.source.trim().length === 0

const dropLeadingBlank = (run: ReadonlyArray<CodePiece>): ReadonlyArray<CodePiece> =>
  Array.dropWhile(run, isBlank)

const dropTrailingBlank = (run: ReadonlyArray<CodePiece>): ReadonlyArray<CodePiece> =>
  pipe(run, Array.reverse, Array.dropWhile(isBlank), Array.reverse)

const trimBlank = (run: ReadonlyArray<CodePiece>): ReadonlyArray<CodePiece> =>
  dropTrailingBlank(dropLeadingBlank(run))

const seamTrailing = (run: ReadonlyArray<CodePiece>): ReadonlyArray<CodePiece> => {
  const lead = dropLeadingBlank(run)
  const body = dropTrailingBlank(lead)
  return Option.match(Array.get(lead, body.length), {
    onNone: () => body,
    onSome: (blank) => [...body, blank],
  })
}

const sealRuns = (
  runs: ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<CodePiece>>,
): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<CodePiece>> =>
  Array.map(runs, (run, i) => (i === runs.length - 1 ? trimBlank(run) : seamTrailing(run)))

const codeRuns = (code: ReadonlyArray<SectionBodyWeft>): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<CodePiece>> =>
  Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(code)
    ? pipe(
        code,
        Array.groupWith((a, _b) => a.type !== 'ArrowWeft'),
        Array.map((block) => Array.getSomes(Array.map(block, pieceOf))),
        Array.filter((run) => Array.some(run, (p) => !isBlank(p))),
        sealRuns,
      )
    : []

const walkRun =
  (bind: (anchor: WarpAnchorToken) => Part) =>
  (pieces: ReadonlyArray<CodePiece>): ReadonlyArray<Part> => {
    const { start, end } = spanOf(pieces)
    const text = sourceOf(pieces)
    const slice = (from: Point, to: Point): string =>
      text.slice(from.offset - start.offset, to.offset - start.offset)
    const fragment = (from: Point, to: Point): ReadonlyArray<Fragment> => {
      const lit = slice(from, to)
      return lit.length === 0 ? [] : [FragmentSchema.make({ text: lit, origin: between(from, to) })]
    }
    const anchors = pipe(
      pieces,
      Array.flatMap((p) => p.anchors),
      Array.sort(byStart),
    )
    const opensAt = (i: number): Point =>
      pipe(
        Array.get(anchors, i - 1),
        Option.match({ onNone: () => start, onSome: (a) => a.position.end }),
      )
    return [
      ...Array.flatMap(anchors, (anchor, i) => [
        ...fragment(opensAt(i), anchor.position.start),
        bind(anchor),
      ]),
      ...fragment(opensAt(anchors.length), end),
    ]
  }

const byStart: Order.Order<WarpAnchorToken> = Order.mapInput(
  Order.Number,
  (a) => a.position.start.offset,
)

## Binding an anchor

`bindAnchor` turns one anchor into one part. A value warp composes its literal, a named section composes an edge to that section, and an anchor that cannot compose leaves an empty fragment in its place. So a broken anchor never leaves a dangling reference: the section always composes, and the product code around the anchor keeps its language service.

A value warp binds first, whatever kind its anchor carries. The anchor composes that warp's text as a `Fragment` where the anchor stood, even when it is marked as a reference, since a literal names no module. The fragment carries the warp's health, so an undeclared `${name}` raises its fault at the warp. No edge is made, since a literal reaches no section.

An anchor of the `Reference` kind names a module, the file another section tangles to, rather than composing a section. It is a reference, written `{Reference}` in its braces, or `kind=Reference` beside other parameters, and it stands where the code names a module, as an import's path does. `isReference` reads the kind, and `referenceOf` binds the reference into a `ModuleRef`. When the document holds one section of the anchor's name, the reference records that section as its target, as a composing edge does. Two such sections make the name ambiguous, and the reference composes nothing. When the document holds none, the target stays open, and the compiler finds the name through the book. The reference also records the file the anchor names in parentheses, if it names one, and the slot just past its closing bracket, where a fix can name one. A reference makes no check of languages, because the code it names stays in its own file. A TypeScript import may name a JSON module.

The author marks a reference, because Loom treats code as opaque text and cannot tell where a path goes. An anchor that carries no such kind composes, wherever it stands. Only a reference names a file, so an anchor that composes, yet writes a file in parentheses after its name, as `::[Saying hello](greeting.loom)` does, composes nothing. Its empty fragment carries the `StrayTarget` fault on the parentheses, whose fix removes them. `stray` builds that health.

Any other anchor composes a section, and its name is looked up among the document's section titles. One match resolves to a `NameRef`, the edge that carries the target section's identity, and the projection follows it and inlines the section there. `localHealth` marks the edge in three cases. A match that holds no code yet is a section to be settled, and the edge carries the `UnsettledSection` note. A match in another language is a cross-language transclusion. The edge stands, since the section is real, but the pass marks the anchor, because a JSON section composed into a TypeScript one shares no file. A match that holds code, reached by an anchor still labelled `{To be settled}`, carries the `SettledLabel` note, whose fix removes the label.

An anchor marks its section to be settled in its braces, in any case of letters. It writes the kind alone, as `{To be settled}`, or in full beside other parameters, as `{kind="To be settled" provides=greet}`. `settleKindOf` reads the span that marks it, and every edge the pass makes records that span, so the judgement and the tangle know which names the author meant before writing them. The span is what a settled section's fix removes. For the kind alone it is the whole of the braces. For the kind in full it is the `kind` parameter with the space that parts it from its neighbour, so the other parameters stay. `parameterSpanOf` finds that span.

An anchor that states the names its section provides, as `{provides=greet}` does, promises the section as well. `providesOf` reads those names, and every edge records them beside the span, so a name the book does not carry yet is meant either way.

A name that no section of the document carries may still lie elsewhere in the book. `openRef` composes an edge for it that records the name and leaves the target open, and the compiler binds that edge once it holds the corpus.

An anchor that cannot compose leaves an empty fragment instead, and what rides on that fragment depends on why it failed. A grammatically broken anchor, such as an empty `::[]` or an unclosed `::[n`, already carries the parser's fault on its own span, so the fragment adds none. A well-formed name that two sections of the document share is ambiguous, and the fragment carries that fault on the anchor. No path tells two sections of one loom apart, so the fault offers none.

=>

const bindAnchor =
  (
    index: NameIndex,
    values: ReadonlyMap<string, ValueWarp>,
    host: string,
    modulePath: string,
  ) =>
  (anchor: WarpAnchorToken): Part => {
    const name = anchor.name.value
    const at = anchor.name.position
    if (anchor.health.status !== 'ok') return blankFragment(anchor.position)
    const value = values.get(name)
    const refersHere = value === undefined && isReference(anchor)
    if (anchor.target !== undefined && !refersHere) {
      return blankFragment(anchor.position, stray(anchor.target))
    }
    const label = settleKindOf(anchor)
    const provides = providesOf(anchor)
    if (value !== undefined) {
      return FragmentSchema.make({ text: value.text, origin: at, health: value.health })
    }
    if (refersHere) return referenceOf(index, modulePath, anchor, label, provides)
    return Array.matchLeft(index.get(name) ?? [], {
      onEmpty: () => openRef(name, at, label, provides),
      onNonEmpty: (entry, rest) =>
        rest.length === 0
          ? NameRefSchema.make({
              name,
              target: Option.some<SectionId>({
                path: modulePath,
                name: entry.name,
              }),
              anchor: at,
              toBeSettled: label,
              provides,
              health: localHealth(entry, name, at, host, label),
            })
          : blankFragment(anchor.position, ambiguous(name, at, rest.length + 1, [])),
    })
  }

const referenceOf = (
  index: NameIndex,
  modulePath: string,
  anchor: WarpAnchorToken,
  label: Option.Option<Position>,
  provides: ReadonlyArray<Provided>,
): Part => {
  const name = anchor.name.value
  const reference = (target: Option.Option<SectionId>): ModuleRef =>
    ModuleRefSchema.make({
      name,
      target,
      file: Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(anchor.target), (written) => ({
        name: written.value,
        at: written.position,
      })),
      anchor: anchor.name.position,
      slot: between(anchor.close.position.end, anchor.close.position.end),
      toBeSettled: label,
      provides,
    })
  return Array.matchLeft(index.get(name) ?? [], {
    onEmpty: () => reference(Option.none()),
    onNonEmpty: (entry, rest) =>
      rest.length === 0
        ? reference(Option.some({ path: modulePath, name: entry.name }))
        : blankFragment(
            anchor.position,
            ambiguous(name, anchor.name.position, rest.length + 1, []),
          ),
  })
}

const isKind =
  (kind: string) =>
  (value: string): boolean =>
    value.toLowerCase() === kind.toLowerCase()

const isSettleKind = isKind(settleKind)

const isReference = (anchor: WarpAnchorToken): boolean =>
  Match.value(anchor.specifier).pipe(
    Match.when({ type: 'Specifier' }, (specifier) => isKind(referenceKind)(specifier.label.value)),
    Match.when({ type: 'Parameters' }, (specifier) =>
      Array.some(
        specifier.parameters,
        (parameter) =>
          parameter.key.value === 'kind' &&
          Array.some(parameter.values, (kind) => isKind(referenceKind)(kind.value)),
      ),
    ),
    Match.orElse(() => false),
  )

const settleKindOf = (anchor: WarpAnchorToken): Option.Option<Position> =>
  Match.value(anchor.specifier).pipe(
    Match.when({ type: 'Specifier' }, (specifier) =>
      Option.filter(Option.some(specifier.position), () => isSettleKind(specifier.label.value)),
    ),
    Match.when({ type: 'Parameters' }, (specifier) =>
      Option.map(
        Array.findFirstWithIndex(specifier.parameters, isSettleParameter),
        ([own, index]) => parameterSpanOf(specifier, own, index),
      ),
    ),
    Match.orElse(() => Option.none<Position>()),
  )

const isSettleParameter = (parameter: ParameterToken): boolean =>
  parameter.key.value === 'kind' &&
  parameter.values.length === 1 &&
  Array.some(parameter.values, (value) => isSettleKind(value.value))

const parameterSpanOf = (
  specifier: ParametersToken,
  own: ParameterToken,
  index: number,
): Position =>
  pipe(
    Array.get(specifier.parameters, index + 1),
    Option.map((next) => between(own.position.start, next.position.start)),
    Option.orElse(() =>
      Option.map(Array.get(specifier.parameters, index - 1), (previous) =>
        between(previous.position.end, own.position.end),
      ),
    ),
    Option.getOrElse(() => specifier.position),
  )

const providesOf = (anchor: WarpAnchorToken): ReadonlyArray<Provided> =>
  anchor.specifier?.type === 'Parameters'
    ? pipe(
        anchor.specifier.parameters,
        Array.filter((parameter) => parameter.key.value === 'provides'),
        Array.flatMap((parameter) => parameter.values),
        Array.map((value): Provided => ({ name: value.value, at: value.position })),
      )
    : []

const localHealth = (
  entry: NameEntry,
  name: string,
  at: Position,
  host: string,
  label: Option.Option<Position>,
): Health =>
  Match.value({ written: entry.written, sameLanguage: entry.language === host }).pipe(
    Match.when({ written: false }, () => faulty(UnsettledSection({ name, declared: true }), at)),
    Match.when({ sameLanguage: false }, () => crossLanguage(name, at, host, entry.language)),
    Match.orElse(() => settledHealth(name, at, label)),
  )

const settledHealth = (name: string, at: Position, label: Option.Option<Position>): Health =>
  Option.match(label, {
    onNone: () => okHealth,
    onSome: (span) => faulty(SettledLabel({ name, label: span }), at),
  })

const openRef = (
  title: string,
  at: Position,
  label: Option.Option<Position>,
  provides: ReadonlyArray<Provided>,
): NameRef =>
  NameRefSchema.make({
    name: title,
    target: Option.none(),
    anchor: at,
    toBeSettled: label,
    provides,
  })

const blankFragment = (at: Position, health: Health = okHealth): Fragment =>
  FragmentSchema.make({ text: '', origin: at, health })

const unresolved = (name: string, at: Position): Health => faulty(UnresolvedAnchor({ name }), at)

const ambiguous = (
  name: string,
  at: Position,
  count: number,
  paths: ReadonlyArray<string>,
): Health => faulty(AmbiguousAnchor({ name, count, paths }), at)

const crossLanguage = (name: string, at: Position, host: string, found: string): Health =>
  faulty(CrossLanguageAnchor({ name, host, found }), at)

const stray = (target: WarpAnchorTargetToken): Health =>
  faulty(StrayTarget({ target: target.source, at: target.position }), target.position)

## Judging an edge beyond the document

The product pass cannot judge an edge that leaves the document. `crossFileFaults` judges it once the compiler holds the whole corpus. It reads one module's product with the `Book` beside it, and returns a diagnostic for each edge beyond the module that cannot compose. The compiler adds these to the module's own diagnostics. So the editor marks a broken anchor where the author wrote it, and the tangle refuses to write a file that would silently drop one.

Two kinds of edge are worth judging. A composing edge whose target is open goes to `nameHealth`. Every reference goes to `referenceHealth`, whether its target is open or not, since even a section of its own loom must land in a file a path can name. `referenceHealth` turns the `Referred` that the section "Fitting a reference to its place" describes into health. A file found is healthy. A module nobody has written yet carries the `UnsettledModule` note, and a fault carries its own health. The compiler hands in the corpus's `Landing`, built once for every module it judges.

=>

export const crossFileFaults = (
  book: Book,
  modules: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomModule>,
  module: LoomModule,
  landing: Landing,
): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  pipe(
    module.product.code,
    Array.flatMap((host) =>
      Array.flatMap(host.fragments, (part) =>
        Match.value(part).pipe(
          Match.when({ type: 'NameRef' }, (ref) =>
            Option.isNone(ref.target) ? nameHealth(book, modules, module, host, ref).diagnostics : [],
          ),
          Match.when(
            { type: 'ModuleRef' },
            (ref) => referenceHealth(book, modules, landing, module, host, ref).diagnostics,
          ),
          Match.orElse((): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => []),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

const referenceHealth = (
  book: Book,
  modules: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomModule>,
  landing: Landing,
  module: LoomModule,
  host: Code,
  ref: ModuleRef,
): Health =>
  Referred.$match(referredFileOf(book, modules, landing, module, host, ref), {
    Named: () => okHealth,
    Unsettled: ({ declared }) => faulty(UnsettledModule({ name: ref.name, declared }), ref.anchor),
    Faulted: ({ health }) => health,
  })

~

An open edge resolves its name through the book, and `nameHealth` judges what `trailsOf` finds. No trail means that no section carries the name. An anchor labelled `{To be settled}`, or one that states what its section provides, meant exactly that, so it carries the `UnsettledSection` note. Any other anchor is unresolved, and `unresolvedHealth` raises the fault, for a composing edge and a reference alike. The anchor's own loom is the natural place for the missing section, so the fault carries the declaration `declarationBelow` writes. `settleAt` finds the point just past the anchor where the label can go instead, unless the anchor already carries braces of its own. More than one trail is an ambiguity, and `pathsTo` gives the path that names each candidate alone, which the fault offers as fixes. One trail reaches a place, and `trailHealth` judges it.

The place is a section, or a chapter that stands for its opening section, or a box that points to a chapter nobody has written yet. A chapter with no titled section gives the anchor no section to compose, so the anchor is unresolved. A box declares its chapter before the chapter exists, so an anchor that reaches the box carries the `UnsettledSection` note, as for a section declared without code. A section that holds no code yet is to be settled. Reached through the chapter it opens, it raises the `ChapterWithoutCode` warning. Reached by its own title, it carries the `UnsettledSection` note. Any other section must share the host's language, and an anchor that still labels it `{To be settled}` carries the `SettledLabel` note. `codeOf` finds the `Code` a reached section composed. `reachedHealth` asks those questions of that `Code` in turn: whether it is written, whether its language is the host's, and whether the anchor's label has outlived its use.

`declarationBelow` writes the heading that would declare a missing section, at the same level as the heading of the section that holds the anchor. `headingMarkOf` gives that level's run of `#` marks, and a division, which a divider opens, counts as a heading of the second level, as Markdown reads a line underlined with dashes. It goes just above the next section's heading, so the new section follows the one that names it, or at the end of the loom when no section follows. `pointAtEnd` finds that end, as a line, a column, and an offset.

=>

const nameHealth = (
  book: Book,
  modules: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomModule>,
  module: LoomModule,
  host: Code,
  ref: NameRef,
): Health => {
  const trails = trailsOf(book, module.path, ref.name)
  return Array.matchLeft(trails, {
    onEmpty: () =>
      isMeant(ref)
        ? faulty(UnsettledSection({ name: ref.name, declared: false }), ref.anchor)
        : unresolvedHealth(module, host, ref.name, ref.anchor),
    onNonEmpty: (trail, rest) =>
      rest.length > 0
        ? ambiguous(ref.name, ref.anchor, trails.length, pathsTo(book, module.path, trails))
        : trailHealth(modules, host, ref, trail),
  })
}

const unresolvedHealth = (
  module: LoomModule,
  host: Code,
  name: string,
  at: Position,
): Health =>
  faulty(
    UnresolvedAnchor({
      name,
      ...Option.match(declarationBelow(module, host, name), {
        onNone: () => ({}),
        onSome: (declaration) => ({ declaration }),
      }),
      ...Option.match(settleAt(module, at), {
        onNone: () => ({}),
        onSome: (point) => ({ settleAt: point }),
      }),
    }),
    at,
  )

const trailHealth = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomModule>,
  host: Code,
  ref: NameRef,
  trail: Trail,
): Health =>
  Option.match(
    Option.flatMap(sectionOf(trail), (reached) => codeOf(modules, reached)),
    {
      onNone: () =>
        Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place._tag === 'Box'
          ? faulty(UnsettledSection({ name: ref.name, declared: true }), ref.anchor)
          : unresolved(ref.name, ref.anchor),
      onSome: (code) =>
        reachedHealth(
          ref.name,
          ref.anchor,
          host.languageId,
          code,
          Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place._tag === 'Chapter',
          ref.toBeSettled,
        ),
    },
  )

const reachedHealth = (
  name: string,
  at: Position,
  host: string,
  code: Code,
  whole: boolean,
  label: Option.Option<Position>,
): Health =>
  Match.value({
    written: Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(code.fragments),
    whole,
    sameLanguage: code.languageId === host,
  }).pipe(
    Match.when({ written: false, whole: true }, () => faulty(ChapterWithoutCode({ name }), at)),
    Match.when({ written: false }, () => faulty(UnsettledSection({ name, declared: true }), at)),
    Match.when({ sameLanguage: false }, () => crossLanguage(name, at, host, code.languageId)),
    Match.orElse(() => settledHealth(name, at, label)),
  )

const settleAt = (module: LoomModule, at: Position): Option.Option<Position> =>
  pipe(
    module.doc.sections,
    Array.flatMap((section) => Array.flatMap(section.code, (weft) => weft.anchors)),
    Array.findFirst((anchor) => anchor.name.position.start.offset === at.start.offset),
    Option.filter((anchor) => anchor.specifier === undefined),
    Option.map((anchor) => between(anchor.position.end, anchor.position.end)),
  )

const pointAtEnd = (text: string): Point => {
  const lines = text.split('\n')
  return { line: lines.length, column: lines[lines.length - 1]!.length + 1, offset: text.length }
}

const headingMarkOf = (heading: LoomHeading): string =>
  heading.marker.type === 'HeadingStart' ? heading.marker.source.trim() : '##'

const declarationBelow = (
  module: LoomModule,
  host: Code,
  name: string,
): Option.Option<Declaration> =>
  Option.map(
    Array.findFirstIndex(
      module.doc.sections,
      (section) => sectionNameOf(section) === host.origin.name,
    ),
    (index): Declaration => {
      const heading = `${headingMarkOf(module.doc.sections[index]!.heading)} ${name}\n`
      return Option.match(Array.get(module.doc.sections, index + 1), {
        onSome: (next) => ({
          at: between(next.heading.position.start, next.heading.position.start),
          text: `${heading}\n`,
        }),
        onNone: () => ({
          at: between(pointAtEnd(module.text), pointAtEnd(module.text)),
          text: `${module.text.endsWith('\n') ? '\n' : '\n\n'}${heading}`,
        }),
      })
    },
  )

const codeOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomModule>,
  reached: SectionRef,
): Option.Option<Code> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(reached.module)),
    Option.flatMap((there) =>
      Array.findFirst(
        there.product.code,
        (code) => code.origin.name === sectionNameOf(reached.section),
      ),
    ),
  )

~

The projection binds an open edge the same way the judgement resolves it. `targetOf` gives an edge's target: the one the pass recorded, or else the section `resolveName` reaches through the book from the loom that holds the edge.

=>

export const targetOf = (book: Book, from: Location, ref: NameRef): Option.Option<SectionId> =>
  Option.orElse(ref.target, () =>
    Option.map(resolveName(book, from, ref.name), (reached) => ({
      path: reached.module,
      name: sectionNameOf(reached.section),
    })),
  )

## Fitting a reference to its place

A reference names a module, so its name must find something that emits a file. `referredFileOf` finds that file, or the reason there is none, as a `Referred`. A `Named` holds the file, a `Landed` that pairs the file with the loom that emits it. An `Unsettled` says nobody has written the module yet. A `Faulted` holds the health of the fault that stops the reference.

`referredFileOf` works in two steps. The first finds what the name names, and `referredNodeOf` takes it. A reference whose own loom holds a section of its name names that section. Any other reference finds its name through the book, nearest first, as a composing anchor does, and `nodeOfPlace` reads the place where the one trail ends, a chapter or a section. A box whose chapter is not written yet names a module to be settled. A name no trail reaches is unresolved, unless the anchor promises the module, with `{To be settled}` or a `provides` parameter, and then the module is to be settled. A name two trails reach is ambiguous, and a part names no module.

=>

export interface Landed {
  readonly loom: Location
  readonly file: File
}

export type Referred = Data.TaggedEnum<{
  Named: { readonly landed: Landed }
  Unsettled: { readonly declared: boolean }
  Faulted: { readonly health: Health }
}>

export const Referred = Data.taggedEnum<Referred>()

type ReferredNode = Data.TaggedEnum<{
  Chapter: { readonly module: LoomModule }
  Section: { readonly id: SectionId }
}>

const ReferredNode = Data.taggedEnum<ReferredNode>()

export const referredFileOf = (
  book: Book,
  modules: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomModule>,
  landing: Landing,
  module: LoomModule,
  host: Code,
  ref: ModuleRef,
): Referred =>
  Result.match(referredNodeOf(book, module, host, ref), {
    onFailure: (stopped) => stopped,
    onSuccess: (node) =>
      ReferredNode.$match(node, {
        Chapter: ({ module: chapter }) => chapterFile(ref, filesOf(chapter)),
        Section: ({ id }) => sectionFile(modules, landing, host, ref, id),
      }),
  })

const referredNodeOf = (
  book: Book,
  module: LoomModule,
  host: Code,
  ref: ModuleRef,
): Result.Result<ReferredNode, Referred> =>
  Option.match(ref.target, {
    onSome: (id) => Result.succeed(ReferredNode.Section({ id })),
    onNone: () => {
      const trails = trailsOf(book, module.path, ref.name)
      return Array.matchLeft(trails, {
        onEmpty: () =>
          Result.fail(
            isMeant(ref)
              ? Referred.Unsettled({ declared: false })
              : Referred.Faulted({ health: unresolvedHealth(module, host, ref.name, ref.anchor) }),
          ),
        onNonEmpty: (trail, rest) =>
          rest.length > 0
            ? Result.fail(
                Referred.Faulted({
                  health: ambiguous(
                    ref.name,
                    ref.anchor,
                    trails.length,
                    pathsTo(book, module.path, trails),
                  ),
                }),
              )
            : nodeOfPlace(Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place, ref),
      })
    },
  })

const nodeOfPlace = (place: Place, ref: ModuleRef): Result.Result<ReferredNode, Referred> =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) =>
      Result.succeed(ReferredNode.Chapter({ module: chapter.module })),
    ),
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref: reached }) =>
      Result.succeed(
        ReferredNode.Section({
          id: { path: reached.module, name: sectionNameOf(reached.section) },
        }),
      ),
    ),
    Match.tag('Box', () => Result.fail(Referred.Unsettled({ declared: true }))),
    Match.tag('Part', () =>
      Result.fail(Referred.Faulted({ health: unresolved(ref.name, ref.anchor) })),
    ),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

~

The second step fits what the name found to a reference, which takes a file rather than code. A chapter gives the file it emits, and `filesOf` reads a loom's files. A chapter that emits none yet is to be settled, since nobody has written its module yet. A section whose heading carries a sink gives the file the sink writes.

`fileAmong` picks the file from those a chapter or a sink section emits. A reference that names a file in parentheses takes the file of that name, and a file the node does not emit raises `NoSuchFile`. A reference that names no file takes the node's one file, and a node that emits several raises `SeveralFiles`. `isNamed` matches a written name against a file's path, whole or by its last segments, so `room.ts` names `src/room.ts`. `fileNames` names a node's files in a message by the last segment of each path, or by the whole path where two of them share a last segment.

=>

const filesOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Landed> =>
  Array.map(module.product.files, (file): Landed => ({ loom: module.path, file }))

const isNamed = (path: string, written: string): boolean =>
  path === written || path.endsWith(`/${written}`)

const baseName = (path: string): string => path.slice(path.lastIndexOf('/') + 1)

const fileNames = (files: ReadonlyArray<Landed>): ReadonlyArray<string> => {
  const bases = Array.map(files, (landed) => baseName(landed.file.path))
  return Array.map(files, (landed, i) =>
    Array.filter(bases, (base) => base === bases[i]).length > 1
      ? landed.file.path
      : baseName(landed.file.path),
  )
}

const chapterFile = (ref: ModuleRef, files: ReadonlyArray<Landed>): Referred =>
  Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(files)
    ? fileAmong(ref, files)
    : Referred.Unsettled({ declared: true })

const fileAmong = (ref: ModuleRef, files: Array.NonEmptyReadonlyArray<Landed>): Referred =>
  Option.match(ref.file, {
    onSome: (written) =>
      Option.match(
        Array.findFirst(files, (landed) => isNamed(landed.file.path, written.name)),
        {
          onSome: (landed) => Referred.Named({ landed }),
          onNone: () =>
            Referred.Faulted({
              health: faulty(
                NoSuchFile({
                  name: ref.name,
                  file: written.name,
                  files: fileNames(files),
                  at: written.at,
                }),
                ref.anchor,
              ),
            }),
        },
      ),
    onNone: () =>
      files.length === 1
        ? Referred.Named({ landed: Array.headNonEmpty(files) })
        : Referred.Faulted({
            health: faulty(
              SeveralFiles({ name: ref.name, files: fileNames(files), at: ref.slot }),
              ref.anchor,
            ),
          }),
  })

~

A section with no sink gives the one file it lands in, the file of the root that composes it. `sectionFile` tells the two kinds of section apart, and `landedFile` reads a sinkless section's file from the landing. It raises `SectionWithoutFile` when the section lands in no file, in several, or in the very file the reference stands in, since no path can be written for any of them. A sinkless section emits no file of its own, so a file in parentheses names nothing there, and it raises `NoSuchFile`.

`landingOf` builds the landing once for the corpus: for every section, the files whose composition reaches it, keyed by the section's identity. `reachedFrom` walks one file's composition the way the projection inlines it, following each composing edge and never a reference, so it gives every section the file's root composes, the root among them. A corpus that holds no reference needs no landing, so `landingOf` walks nothing for it, and `holdsReferences` tells such a corpus from one that refers. `landedIn` reads the files one section lands in, and `sameFile` tells whether two landings are one file.

=>

export type Landing = Readonly<Record<string, ReadonlyArray<Landed>>>

const landedIn = (landing: Landing, id: SectionId): ReadonlyArray<Landed> =>
  Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(landing[keyOf(id)]), () => [])

const sameFile = (one: Landed, other: Landed): boolean =>
  one.loom === other.loom && one.file.path === other.file.path

const sectionFile = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomModule>,
  landing: Landing,
  host: Code,
  ref: ModuleRef,
  id: SectionId,
): Referred => {
  const own = pipe(
    Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(id.path)), {
      onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Landed> => [],
      onSome: filesOf,
    }),
    Array.filter((landed) => landed.file.code.origin.name === id.name),
  )
  if (Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(own)) return fileAmong(ref, own)
  return Option.match(ref.file, {
    onSome: (written) =>
      Referred.Faulted({
        health: faulty(
          NoSuchFile({ name: ref.name, file: written.name, files: [], at: written.at }),
          ref.anchor,
        ),
      }),
    onNone: () => landedFile(landing, host, ref, id),
  })
}

const landedFile = (landing: Landing, host: Code, ref: ModuleRef, id: SectionId): Referred => {
  const lands = landedIn(landing, id)
  const here = landedIn(landing, host.origin)
  const inHere = (landed: Landed): boolean => Array.some(here, (one) => sameFile(one, landed))
  const sole = lands.length === 1 ? Array.head(lands) : Option.none<Landed>()
  return Option.match(
    Option.filter(sole, (landed) => !inHere(landed)),
    {
      onSome: (landed) => Referred.Named({ landed }),
      onNone: () =>
        Referred.Faulted({
          health: faulty(
            SectionWithoutFile({
              name: ref.name,
              files: fileNames(lands),
              here: Option.exists(sole, inHere),
            }),
            ref.anchor,
          ),
        }),
    },
  )
}

export const holdsReferences = (modules: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomModule>): boolean =>
  Array.some(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) =>
    Array.some(module.product.code, (code) =>
      Array.some(code.fragments, (part) => part.type === 'ModuleRef'),
    ),
  )

const codeAt = (modules: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomModule>, id: SectionId): Option.Option<Code> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(id.path)),
    Option.flatMap((there) => Array.findFirst(there.product.code, (code) => code.origin.name === id.name)),
  )

const reachedFrom = (
  book: Book,
  modules: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomModule>,
  root: Code,
): ReadonlyArray<string> => {
  const walk = (seen: ReadonlySet<string>, code: Code): ReadonlySet<string> =>
    Array.reduce(code.fragments, seen, (reached, part) =>
      part.type !== 'NameRef'
        ? reached
        : pipe(
            targetOf(book, code.origin.path, part),
            Option.filter((target) => !reached.has(keyOf(target))),
            Option.flatMap((target) =>
              Option.map(codeAt(modules, target), (next) =>
                walk(new Set([...reached, keyOf(target)]), next),
              ),
            ),
            Option.getOrElse(() => reached),
          ),
    )
  return Array.fromIterable(walk(new Set([keyOf(root.origin)]), root))
}

export const landingOf = (book: Book, modules: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomModule>): Landing =>
  holdsReferences(modules)
    ? pipe(
        Array.fromIterable(modules.values()),
        Array.flatMap(filesOf),
        Array.flatMap((landed) =>
          Array.map(reachedFrom(book, modules, landed.file.code), (key) => ({ key, landed })),
        ),
        Array.groupBy((entry) => entry.key),
        Record.map((entries) => Array.map(entries, (entry) => entry.landed)),
      )
    : {}

## A section's name and its spans

A section's name is its heading title normalised to an identifier, through the tokeniser's `normaliseTitle` — `Multiplier Function` becomes `MultiplierFunction`. Every heading has a title, so the function is total, and the name pairs with the module path to make the section's corpus-wide identity. The formatter reads the same name, to pair a section with the `Code` the pass builds for it.

The span helpers read a run's source and bounds. `sourceOf` joins a piece list's source, `spanOf` takes the span from its first and last positions, and `between` pairs two points into a span.

=>

export const sectionNameOf = (section: LoomSection): string =>
  normaliseTitle(section.heading.title?.source ?? '')

const sourceOf = (nodes: ReadonlyArray<{ readonly source: string }>): string =>
  nodes.map((n) => n.source).join('')

const spanOf = (nodes: ReadonlyArray<{ readonly position: Position }>): Position => ({
  start: nodes[0]!.position.start,
  end: nodes[nodes.length - 1]!.position.end,
})

const between = (start: Point, end: Point): Position => ({ start, end })

## One name, one section

A section's name is its identity within its loom, so two sections of one loom may not share it. The editor gives each section's composed code its name in lower case, because Volar's ids are lower case. So two titles that differ only in case or punctuation, such as `Saying hello` and `Saying Hello!`, fold to one identity, and `identityOf` gives that folded form. A section titled like the chapter heading above it collides too, because that heading is a section of its own.

`firstHolders` maps each identity to the first section in the document that holds it. It builds the map from the sections in reverse, so the first holder of a name overwrites every later one. `duplicateHealth` marks each later holder with the `DuplicateName` fault, on its own title, and names the line where the first holder's heading stands. A section with no title holds no name, so it takes part on neither side.

The pass puts that health on the later section's `Code`. The editor then marks the heading, and the tangle refuses the loom. Without the fault, a lookup by name would find the first holder for both, so a sink could compose its namesake's code, or nothing at all.

=>

const identityOf = (section: LoomSection): string => sectionNameOf(section).toLowerCase()

const firstHolders = (sections: ReadonlyArray<LoomSection>): ReadonlyMap<string, LoomSection> =>
  new Map(
    pipe(
      sections,
      Array.filter((section) => section.heading.title !== undefined),
      Array.map((section) => [identityOf(section), section] as const),
      Array.reverse,
    ),
  )

const duplicateHealth = (first: ReadonlyMap<string, LoomSection>, section: LoomSection): Health =>
  pipe(
    Option.all([
      Option.fromNullishOr(section.heading.title),
      pipe(
        Option.fromNullishOr(first.get(identityOf(section))),
        Option.filter((holder) => holder !== section),
      ),
    ]),
    Option.map(([title, holder]) =>
      faulty(
        DuplicateName({
          name: title.source,
          sibling: 'section',
          where: String(holder.heading.position.start.line),
        }),
        title.position,
      ),
    ),
    Option.getOrElse(() => okHealth),
  )

## A fence's label

Fenced code may wear a label after its opening backticks, the language name Markdown writes there for any viewer that colours it, or leave it off, since Loom already knows the section's language. `sectionLanguage` gives it: the heading's specifier first, then the sink's extension, then the chapter. So a label tells Loom nothing new. It must agree with that language, the specifier's above all, or one block would have two languages. A fence with no label has nothing to check.

A label agrees when it names the section's language, or a file extension that maps to it. `extensionLanguage` already maps an extension to a language for sinks, and a bare name passes through it unchanged, so `ts` and `typescript` both agree with a TypeScript section. `labelsOf` gathers a section's fence labels, and `labelHealth` gives the first label that disagrees a `FenceLabelAgainstLanguage` fault, on the label itself.

A section's code carries one health. `withHealth` folds a second check into it, keeping the worse status and both diagnostics, so `buildSection` can hold a section to a single name and its fences to its language at once.

=>

const labelsOf = (section: LoomSection): ReadonlyArray<FenceLabelToken> =>
  Array.flatMap(section.code, (weft) =>
    weft.type === 'FenceWeft' && weft.label !== undefined ? [weft.label] : [],
  )

const labelHealth = (section: LoomSection, languageId: string): Health =>
  Option.match(
    Array.findFirst(labelsOf(section), (label) => extensionLanguage(label.source) !== languageId),
    {
      onNone: () => okHealth,
      onSome: (label) =>
        faulty(
          FenceLabelAgainstLanguage({ label: label.source, language: languageId }),
          label.position,
        ),
    },
  )

const withHealth = (first: Health, second: Health): Health => ({
  status: first.status === 'ok' ? second.status : first.status,
  diagnostics: [...first.diagnostics, ...second.diagnostics],
})

## The module {Tangle}

The tangle composes the sections in the source's own order — the spine first, then the helpers it reaches for, each section named by its title, the local anchor form Loom prefers.

